foreign  customers are waiting for trucks and  SUVs just like these and many have sat  on that wait list for more than a year  currently we're in body shop this is our  one body shop that we're looking at  right now the electric vehicle company  is now burning through cash rushing to  fill those orders it's a startup trying  to prove it can build vehicles at scale  without having ramped up before it  definitely pressure that's felt that  felt on the floor it's felt by the  entire team so what is the company doing  to build faster and can it become what  investors want it to be the Tesla of  trucks before its competition I toured  rivian's plant and visited Ford's EV  facility to find out  rivian's Factory in Normal Illinois was  previously home to Mitsubishi which  built cars here until 2015. remnants of  that facility like a large part stamping  machine remain  but much of the plant in the process for  making rivian's three vehicles the r1t  r1s and Amazon branded commercial van  are new  as is Tim Fallon who was brought into  rivien in February after 16 years at  Nissan what's job one right now job one  right now is is  um I'll say I don't want to say  finishing the ramp outside continuing  the ramp and getting to stability may be  defined for us what stability means in  us in a factory stability to me is I  want I want people I want all of my team  members technicians to come in and see  the exact same thing they saw yesterday  the parts in the same place the right  number of Parts everything running  appropriately and really being able to  get into that Rhythm and really do the  dance of building vehicles that starts  with stamping from there robots piece  together the parts to form the body This  is highly automated all of the welding  is automated the trucks eventually end  here the general assembly line where  items including seats are locked into  place  getting this entire process to run  smoothly has been a challenge for  ribbian  earlier this year manufacturing issues  sometimes left workers idle for hours  looking back I mean we'd hear about  issues with parts you know sequencing  essentially like not being able to get  the right parts to the right place on  the line at the right time I'm wondering  how you handle that if it's fixed where  you're at in that process there's no  doubt there were there were significant  issues in the past and there are still  some issues that we're working through  but they've gotten significantly better  and you know as we continue to audit our  processes and we continue to refine  those processes and get better we  definitely see fewer and fewer  interruptions to our production because  of it a snarled supply chain and  inflationary pressures have made this  ramp even more challenging Fallon says  rivian in March cut its production  guidance to 25 000 vehicles for the year  last quarter It produced seven thousand  which means it must now build more than  10 000 Vehicles this quarter to hit its  Target  rivien says it's on track to hit its  goal but this Factory has the capacity  to produce six times that 150 000  Vehicles a year  to get there rivian says it's bringing  in more equipment and has recently added  a second shift throughout the facility  can you boil it down to what it is that  would keep you from instead of doing 25  000 this year doing doing 50 000. it's  the working with our supply chain and  then also the ability to get the second  shift on and really get everything  ramped up to that second shift so that  increasing productivity that we'll see  throughout the rest of this year and  into next year will allow us to  consistently be able to get the vehicles  out to our customers at a higher rate  than we have been and then we're looking  at that what is the capacity that we  need to be at to continue to fulfill  that demand that hopefully will continue  to be ever increasing demand for our  vehicles  after the factory tour we borrowed an  r1t to travel back to Detroit a couple  stops to charge made it a nine hour trip  giving me plenty of time to reflect on  what I saw my impression from being  there is the place is super busy but  also you know I don't think the lines  moving at a rate that you'd see in a  traditional Auto Plant you can tell it's  a it's a young Factory that's still  going through some Growing Pains but I  think they feel pretty confident in the  fact that they can scale up from here  rivian CEO RJ scaringe has said he  eventually aims to sell 10 million  Vehicles a year around the world similar  to Toyota today  getting there will mean getting that  plant and future facilities to full  capacity  it's a very different challenge to build  a couple a week versus to build a  hundred it's a very different challenge  to build 100 a week versus 100 a day and  so getting past the first stage doesn't  necessarily prepare you for the second  stage of more higher volume production  ribbian is spending heavily to try to  get there reporting a total net loss to  5 billion dollars in the first three  quarters of this year  if you're an investor in Rivier and you  want to see market share you want to see  a return on investment and so getting a  foothold in the market and establishing  yourself as the leader in EV trucks has  to be the goal for them  rivian was the first EV truck to Market  but it's no longer buyers only option  Ford the leader in gas power pickups is  now selling ITV the F-150 Lightning from  the moment we opened up lightning for  reservations we saw a huge in Rush of  customers we got over 200 000  reservations before we close the  reservation book Ford started delivering  trucks to reservation holders in May and  has so far sold around 13 000 Lightnings  including 2000 in November the company's  Target 150  000 truck annual run rate by the end of  next year or about 12 500 a month  Palmer says that's multiple times the  amount Ford originally planned what  specific actions are you taking to get  to that number the first one is we had  to expand the building size then we have  to reconfigure the lines to run at a  much much higher speed and we of course  had to get the materials for everything  some of Ford's efforts like adding new  equipment hundreds of workers and  pushing speed mirror the moves rivian is  making to boost output but Ford isn't a  startup  it's ramp to production many times  before and next door to its EV facility  is a plant cranking out gas engine  F-150s a new truck rolls off the line  here every 53 seconds my asset has  really been advantageous that we have a  very high speed production plan next  door that builds many of the components  for F-150 especially the aluminum body  we would probably never have been able  to increase So Many Factors speed if we  didn't have the plant next door to rely  on but those shared resources aren't  enough to make an entire electric truck  and Ford has grappled with its own  supply chain constraints you may be  aware of the chip shortage that the  whole industry has been facing that's a  real challenge for us and although we do  prioritize the electric vehicle that's  still a challenge for us when we're  trying to ramp up to such a fast rate  deciding how to allocate chips not to  mention cash between EVs and internal  combustion engine Vehicles is unique to  the traditional automakers the challenge  is more than just the development of the  vehicles and the getting parts to it  it's making this bet on will you be  successful in these if you're  traditional you're taking resources away  from things that are making good money  now to go towards this if you're a  startup you've got very limited  resources and you're putting big bets on  this and to not take those bets almost  guarantees that you lose right now Tesla  is the model for a U.S company  manufacturing EVS at scale It produced  nearly 366 000 vehicles in the third  quarter alone Tesla's cars have  accounted for roughly 68 percent of EVS  sold so far this year in the U.S the  nice thing is the Tesla can't make  enough Vehicles either and so you have  opportunities there you're not going  into a zero-sum game the pie is growing  yeah and Tesla doesn't yet have a truck  like the r1t giving rivien a chance to  establish itself as the go-to name in  the category and to keep investor in  consumer dollars coming in we've talked  to a number of consumers who have their  name on  four different lists they're just trying  to get an electric truck and so it  really is at least in these early days  sort of a race to Market  rivian and Ford's ability to speed up  production and pull ahead of the  competition will impact which vehicles  customers drive or don't drive in the  future  [Music]

